In the war, Indians had **helped** Britain and hoped for **self-rule**. Instead, in **March 1919**, came the **Rowlatt Act**. It allowed:

- **arrest without warrant**;
- trial **in secret** (in camera);
- limits on people's **movement**;
- suspension of **habeas corpus** (the right to be brought before a court).

Gandhi led the **Rowlatt Satyagraha**: the **first all-India** struggle, with strong **Hindu-Muslim unity**.

The British minister Lord Birkenhead said Indians could not **agree** on any plan.

An **All Parties Conference** answered him. A committee under **Motilal Nehru** wrote the **Nehru Report (1928)**:
- **Dominion status** for India;
- **joint electorates**, with reserved seats for minorities;
- provinces on a **language** basis;
- **nineteen fundamental rights**, including the vote.

The Congress told Britain: accept it **within a year**, or face a mass movement.

Britain did not accept the report. At **Lahore (December 1929)**, under **Jawaharlal Nehru**:

- the Congress declared **Poorna Swaraj** (**complete independence**) its goal;
- Nehru hoisted the **tricolour** on the bank of the **Ravi** at midnight;
- **26 January 1930** was kept as **Independence Day**;
- **Gandhi** was given power to start **Civil Disobedience**.

That is why **26 January** later became **Republic Day**.
